  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 210 PM Wednesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... The potential for record heat continues through \nFriday.\n\nAs of 2pm, RDU had already warmed to 88 degrees, beating the \nprevious daily record high of 86 set in 1985. In addition, GSO had \nwarmed to 85 degrees, tying the daily record high set in 1975. FAY \nis 87 degrees and will flirt with their daily record high of 90 \ndegrees. This is primarily due to an upper-level ridge building \nacross the southeastern United States, considering surface wind has \nbeen minimal out of the southwest today. The potential for record \nheat on Thursday may be tempered by a cold front currently near the \nMason-Dixon line that will drop south tomorrow into Virginia, and \nsome models are showing this front drifting into northeastern North \nCarolina. There will likely be a 10 degree difference in high \ntemperatures between Raleigh and Roanoke Rapids Thursday afternoon, \nalthough all three climate sites (Greensboro, RDU, Fayetteville) are \nexpected to remain on the warm side of the front. Precipitation \nchances with this front are near zero, but the front will retreat to \nthe north on Friday, allowing the entire forecast area to return to \nthe mid to upper 80s for highs. Along with widespread sunshine on \nFriday, wind gusts as high as 30 mph can be expected in the \nafternoon.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... A cold front Friday night brings a decisive end to \nthe record heat, bringing in well below normal temperatures for the \nweekend and the potential for patchy frost by Sunday morning.\n\nThe cold front is expected to arrive slightly later than the \nprevious forecast, and should now be crossing central North Carolina \nafter sunset Friday evening. Although winds could be gusty with the \nfrontal passage itself, this timing will reduce the overall wind \ngusts due to less mixing in the overnight hours. Models are showing \na noticeable increase for the chance of rain with the frontal \npassage, specifically looking at Saturday morning, although rainfall \ntotals would not be heavy. Saturday's highs will be 20 to 25 degrees \ncolder than Friday, with isolated colder spots only rising into the \nupper 50s, with most locations reaching the low to mid 60s. Saturday \nand Sunday nights should have similar low temperatures, with most \nlocations falling into the 40s, but some sheltered locations \ndropping into the upper 30s with the potential for some frost. \nTemperatures should return to near normal values by the beginning of \nnext week, with highs around 70 and lows around 50.",
